            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-107496",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose gro ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose group order\nis 1 may cause CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() to loop indefinitely.\n\nImpact summary: An attacker able to supply the recipient certificate to an\napplication performing CMS encryption can cause that application to loop\nindefinitely, consuming CPU and resulting in Denial of Service.\n\nCWE: CWE-835: Loop with Unreachable Exit Condition ('Infinite Loop')\n\nDescription: CMS_encrypt() and CMS_encrypt_ex() encrypt a message to one or\nmore recipient certificates and, for an EC recipient, generate an ephemeral\nkey in the recipient's group. If the certificate declares a group order of 1,\nrandom scalar generation can only produce zero. The key generation routine\nthen retries without limit while waiting for a nonzero private scalar, so the\ncall never returns.\n\nThe OpenSSL TLS and QUIC implementations do not use this path. An application\nis exposed only if it calls CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() with a recipient\ncertificate whose EC parameters are attacker-controlled. Certificates with\nnormal EC group orders do not trigger the loop.\n\nOpenSSL 4.0 and later reject unknown explicit EC groups in default builds.\nThese versions are affected only when configured with the\nenable-ec_explicit_curves option.\n\nThe result is a hang that consumes a CPU core until the process is\ninterrupted, a Denial of Service. The severity is Low because the attacker\nmust supply the recipient certificate and the application must choose to\nencrypt to it; ordinary CMS encryption to trusted recipients is unaffected.\n\nThe fix makes EC key generation fail when the group has no valid nonzero\nprivate scalar, instead of retrying indefinitely.\n\nFIPS impact: no\nThe FIPS modules in 4.1, 4.0, 3.5 and 3.0 are not affected. The FIPS provider\ndoes not support arbitrary explicit EC domain parameters, so an order-1 group\nis rejected during key import before the vulnerable key generation path is\nreached."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-107496",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-107496\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: openssl-provider-legacy\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-107496](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-107496)\nIssue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose group order\nis 1 may cause CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() to loop indefinitely.\n\nImpact summary: An attacker able to supply the recipient certificate to an\napplication performing CMS encryption can cause that application to loop\nindefinitely, consuming CPU and resulting in Denial of Service.\n\nCWE: CWE-835: Loop with Unreachable Exit Condition ('Infinite Loop')\n\nDescription: CMS_encrypt() and CMS_encrypt_ex() encrypt a message to one or\nmore recipient certificates and, for an EC recipient, generate an ephemeral\nkey in the recipient's group. If the certificate declares a group order of 1,\nrandom scalar generation can only produce zero. The key generation routine\nthen retries without limit while waiting for a nonzero private scalar, so the\ncall never returns.\n\nThe OpenSSL TLS and QUIC implementations do not use this path. An application\nis exposed only if it calls CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() with a recipient\ncertificate whose EC parameters are attacker-controlled. Certificates with\nnormal EC group orders do not trigger the loop.\n\nOpenSSL 4.0 and later reject unknown explicit EC groups in default builds.\nThese versions are affected only when configured with the\nenable-ec_explicit_curves option.\n\nThe result is a hang that consumes a CPU core until the process is\ninterrupted, a Denial of Service. The severity is Low because the attacker\nmust supply the recipient certificate and the application must choose to\nencrypt to it; ordinary CMS encryption to trusted recipients is unaffected.\n\nThe fix makes EC key generation fail when the group has no valid nonzero\nprivate scalar, instead of retrying indefinitely.\n\nFIPS impact: no\nThe FIPS modules in 4.1, 4.0, 3.5 and 3.0 are not affected. The FIPS provider\ndoes not support arbitrary explicit EC domain parameters, so an order-1 group\nis rejected during key import before the vulnerable key generation path is\nreached.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-107496**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|openssl-provider-legacy||[CVE-2026-107496](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-107496)|\n\nIssue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose group order\nis 1 may cause CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() to loop indefinitely.\n\nImpact summary: An attacker able to supply the recipient certificate to an\napplication performing CMS encryption can cause that application to loop\nindefinitely, consuming CPU and resulting in Denial of Service.\n\nCWE: CWE-835: Loop with Unreachable Exit Condition ('Infinite Loop')\n\nDescription: CMS_encrypt() and CMS_encrypt_ex() encrypt a message to one or\nmore recipient certificates and, for an EC recipient, generate an ephemeral\nkey in the recipient's group. If the certificate declares a group order of 1,\nrandom scalar generation can only produce zero. The key generation routine\nthen retries without limit while waiting for a nonzero private scalar, so the\ncall never returns.\n\nThe OpenSSL TLS and QUIC implementations do not use this path. An application\nis exposed only if it calls CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() with a recipient\ncertificate whose EC parameters are attacker-controlled. Certificates with\nnormal EC group orders do not trigger the loop.\n\nOpenSSL 4.0 and later reject unknown explicit EC groups in default builds.\nThese versions are affected only when configured with the\nenable-ec_explicit_curves option.\n\nThe result is a hang that consumes a CPU core until the process is\ninterrupted, a Denial of Service. The severity is Low because the attacker\nmust supply the recipient certificate and the application must choose to\nencrypt to it; ordinary CMS encryption to trusted recipients is unaffected.\n\nThe fix makes EC key generation fail when the group has no valid nonzero\nprivate scalar, instead of retrying indefinitely.\n\nFIPS impact: no\nThe FIPS modules in 4.1, 4.0, 3.5 and 3.0 are not affected. The FIPS provider\ndoes not support arbitrary explicit EC domain parameters, so an order-1 group\nis rejected during key import before the vulnerable key generation path is\nreached."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
